<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oberkommando der Wehrmacht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as OKW, High Command of the Armed Forces</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oberkommando der <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> (OKW), or High Command of the Armed Forces, was created by Hitler in February 1938 when he abolished the war ministry and took personal command of the German armed forces. Headed by Wilhelm Keitel, with Alfred Jodl as chief of operations, it served as Hitler's military staff and from 1942 directed every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> except the Eastern Front.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oberkommando-der-wehrmacht.jpg" alt="Oberkommando der Wehrmacht" width="260" height="260" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_Oberkommando_der_Wehrmacht_W0224206.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The OKW was set up by executive decree on 4 February 1938, after the Blomberg-Fritsch affair led to the dismissal of Werner von Blomberg, Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces and head of the Reich Ministry of War, and of Werner von Fritsch, Commander-in-Chief of the Army.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, who had been waiting for a chance to gain personal control of the military, used the powers granted to him by the Enabling Act to dissolve the ministry and replace it with the OKW, directly subordinate to him as Supreme Commander of the Armed Forces. Field Marshal Wilhelm Keitel was its chief, with the rank of a Reich Minister, and Lieutenant General Alfred Jodl was Chief of Operations Staff. Many officers disregarded Keitel, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>, Commander-in-Chief of the Air Force, circumvented him to reach Hitler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>By June 1938 the OKW had four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> operations staff under Jodl, the Wehrmacht propaganda troops under General Hasso von Wedel, which numbered 15,000 men at their peak in 1942, and the Amt Ausland/Abwehr, which handled foreign intelligence and counter-intelligence. The OKW had nominal oversight of the high commands of the army, navy and air force, but there was no direct chain of command between it and them. With the outbreak of war it also exercised tactical control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Rivalry with the services, mainly the Army High Command (OKH), kept the OKW from becoming a unified general staff. In practice Hitler used it as his personal military staff, turning his ideas into orders such as the Führer Directives. Norway was the first OKW war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and by 1942 the OKW directed the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, while the OKH held the Eastern Front.</p><p>Hitler set the commands against each other so that military decisions stayed in his hands, at times down to the battalion level, which with delays and his growing indecision helped bring about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s defeat. On 19 December 1941 he dismissed Walther von Brauchitsch and took over command of the army himself. Only on 28 April 1945 did he place the OKH directly under the OKW.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> the OKW was indicted but acquitted of being a criminal organisation, because the Tribunal held that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> and High Command was neither an organisation nor a group within the Charter. Keitel and Jodl were nevertheless convicted of war crimes and sentenced to death by hanging. In the High Command Trial of 1947–48 fourteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> officers were charged, especially over the Commissar Order, the killing of prisoners of war and participation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oberkommando der Wehrmacht” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
